 ## Overview
-Piecewise Quadratic Hermite Interpolation is a method for interpolating a normalized scalar field within a 1D interval using two connected quadratic Bézier curves, ensuring $C^1$ continuity while respecting Hermite data (value and derivative) at both endpoints. It solves the incompatibility of single quadratic interpolation with four constraints (two values, two derivatives) by splitting the interval, providing a numerically stable and efficient alternative to cubic interpolation for root isolation in volume rendering [S1]. It is specifically designed for use within the SCALIS framework, where it operates on a normalized field $g \circ f \circ r^{-1}$ to improve convergence for small numbers of subdivisions compared to cubic interpolation on the original field [S1].
+Piecewise Quadratic Hermite Interpolation is a method for interpolating a normalized scalar field within a 1D interval using two connected quadratic Bézier curves, ensuring continuity and smoothness while respecting Hermite data (value and derivative) at both endpoints [S1]. It solves the incompatibility of single quadratic interpolation with four constraints (two values, two derivatives) by splitting the interval, providing a numerically stable and efficient alternative to cubic interpolation for root isolation in volume rendering [S1]. It is specifically designed for use within the SCALIS framework, where it operates on a normalized field $g \circ f \circ r^{-1}$ to improve convergence for small numbers of subdivisions compared to cubic interpolation on the original field [S1].
 
